{# ---------- Switch automatically ---------- #}
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">AutoSwitch moves these for you</p>
    <h2>Switch automatically</h2>
    <ul class="kinds">
      <li class="kind kind--yes" id="pairing">
        <svg class="kind__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><rect x="7" y="9" width="34" height="27" rx="6"/><path d="M15 40h18"/></svg>
        <h3>Hands off by pairing</h3>
        <p class="kind__one">One pairing at a time. AutoSwitch removes it on the computer you leave and pairs on the one you arrive at.</p>
        <ul class="tags"><li>Magic Trackpad</li><li>Magic Keyboard</li><li>Magic Mouse</li></ul>
        <p class="kind__do"><strong>Setting:</strong> Manage pairing on</p>
        <details class="kind__why">
          <summary>Why it works</summary>
          <p>The device holds one pairing. As soon as one computer lets go, the next can pair without you touching the device.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--yes" id="channel">
        <svg class="kind__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><rect x="5" y="14" width="38" height="22" rx="5"/><path d="M12 22h0M18 22h0M24 22h0M30 22h0M36 22h0M15 29h18"/><circle cx="14" cy="8" r="2.6"/><circle cx="24" cy="8" r="2.6"/><circle cx="34" cy="8" r="2.6"/></svg>
        <h3>Switches channel on request</h3>
        <p class="kind__one">A channel per computer. AutoSwitch tells the device to hop to the other computer's channel, and finds that channel itself.</p>
        <ul class="tags"><li>Logitech K380</li><li>Other Easy-Switch keyboards &amp; mice</li></ul>
        <p class="kind__do"><strong>Setting:</strong> Manage pairing off</p>
        <details class="kind__why">
          <summary>Why it works</summary>
          <p>Logitech's Easy-Switch devices accept a "change host" command from the computer they're connected to. Pairings stay exactly as they are. AutoSwitch checks that your device supports it, and on the Mac it asks once for Input Monitoring.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--partly" id="connects">
        <svg class="kind__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><path d="M9 30v-6a15 15 0 0 1 30 0v6"/><rect x="6" y="28" width="8" height="12" rx="3"/><rect x="34" y="28" width="8" height="12" rx="3"/></svg>
        <h3>Joins whichever computer connects</h3>
        <p class="kind__one">Remembers several computers and goes to the one that asks. The Mac app connects and disconnects it with the KVM.</p>
        <ul class="tags"><li>Many headphones</li><li>Speakers</li></ul>
        <p class="kind__do"><strong>Mac:</strong> supported, being tested · <strong>Windows:</strong> not yet</p>
        <details class="kind__why">
          <summary>Why not on Windows</summary>
          <p>Windows has no way for an app to disconnect a Bluetooth device without unpairing it, which would make you pair it again.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Can't be switched ---------- #}
<section class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">No app can move these</p>
    <h2>Can't be switched by an app</h2>
    <ul class="kinds kinds--two">
      <li class="kind kind--no" id="picks">
        <svg class="kind__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><rect x="5" y="14" width="38" height="22" rx="5"/><path d="M12 22h0M18 22h0M24 22h0M30 22h0M36 22h0M15 29h18"/><path d="M33 6l4 4-4 4"/></svg>
        <h3>Picks the computer itself</h3>
        <p class="kind__one">A pairing per computer, and a key on the device chooses one. Only the device can switch.</p>
        <ul class="tags"><li>ZMK keyboards</li><li>QMK keyboards</li></ul>
        <p class="kind__do"><strong>What to do:</strong> use its own switch key, or plug it into the KVM by USB. Manage pairing off.</p>
        <details class="kind__why">
          <summary>Why not</summary>
          <p>No computer can change which profile the keyboard uses. Removing the pairing on one computer breaks that profile, and you'd pair it again from the keyboard.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
      <li class="kind kind--no" id="repairs">
        <svg class="kind__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><path d="M14 16h20a9 9 0 0 1 8.7 11.4l-2 7.2a4.5 4.5 0 0 1-7.6 1.8L29 32H19l-4.1 4.4a4.5 4.5 0 0 1-7.6-1.8l-2-7.2A9 9 0 0 1 14 16z"/><path d="M15 22v6M12 25h6"/><circle cx="32" cy="23" r="1.6"/><circle cx="35" cy="27" r="1.6"/></svg>
        <h3>Re-pairs by hand</h3>
        <p class="kind__one">Remembers one Bluetooth computer. Moving it means pressing its pairing button.</p>
        <ul class="tags"><li>Xbox</li><li>PlayStation</li><li>Nintendo Pro</li></ul>
        <p class="kind__do"><strong>What to do:</strong> keep it on one computer, use a cable through the KVM, or a <a href="#tips">USB adapter</a>.</p>
        <details class="kind__why">
          <summary>Why not</summary>
          <p>After a computer removes it, the controller still points at that computer, so the other one can't pair until you press the button.</p>
        </details>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Tips ---------- #}
<section class="section" id="tips">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">No app needed</p>
    <h2>Make it follow the KVM like a wired device</h2>
    <ul class="tips">
      <li class="tip">
        <svg class="tip__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><rect x="16" y="6" width="16" height="22" rx="3"/><path d="M20 28v8h8v-8M24 36v6"/><path d="M21 12h6"/></svg>
        <div>
          <h3>USB receiver? Plug it into the KVM</h3>
          <p>Logitech Unifying, Bolt and Lightspeed receivers follow the KVM on their own, and they're faster than Bluetooth. Use the KVM's plain USB port, not a "keyboard/mouse emulation" port.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="tip">
        <svg class="tip__icon" aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 48 48"><path d="M17 15l14 14-7 7V12l7 7-14 14"/></svg>
        <div>
          <h3>Bluetooth-to-USB adapter</h3>
          <p>Pair the device once with an adapter on the KVM. For controllers: the 8BitDo USB Wireless Adapter 2 (Windows and Mac). For keyboards and mice: DIY bridges like HID Remapper. Not for a Magic Trackpad's gestures.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

{# ---------- Test ---------- #}
<section class="section section--tint">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not sure?</p>
    <h2>Test your device in a minute</h2>
    <ol class="steps steps--row">
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">1</span>
        <h3>Connect it to one computer</h3>
        <p>Make sure it works there.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">2</span>
        <h3>Remove it there</h3>
        <p>In Bluetooth settings, remove (forget) it.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__badge">3</span>
        <h3>Pair it on the other one</h3>
        <p>Without pressing anything on the device.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <ul class="outcomes">
      <li class="outcome outcome--yes"><strong>It pairs</strong> Hands off by pairing: AutoSwitch can switch it.</li>
      <li class="outcome outcome--no"><strong>Only after a button press</strong> Re-pairs by hand.</li>
      <li class="outcome outcome--no"><strong>It has a computer-switch key</strong> Picks the computer itself (or, Logitech, switches channel).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="glance__note">
      AutoSwitch guesses the kind from the device's maker and type, and marks a guess as <em>likely</em>.
      Found a device it gets wrong? <a href="{{ site.issues }}">Tell us</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a class="btn btn--primary" href="/#download">Get AutoSwitch KVM</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
